#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720163 :question/problem "Evaluate the scalar line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(2 x^{2} + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is given by $y=- 2 x - 1$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$." :question/worked-solution "Set $x=t$ and use the explicit equation to parametrize the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,- 2 t - 1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nDifferentiate and calculate the nonnegative speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle1,-2\\rangle\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(-2\\right)^2}=\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=f\\left(t,- 2 t - 1\\right)=2 t^{2} - 4 t - 2\n$$\n\nInclude the speed factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{2} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(2 t^{2} - 4 t - 2\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplify the product before integrating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{2}\\left(2 \\sqrt{5} \\left(t^{2} - 2 t - 1\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[\\sqrt{5} \\left(\\frac{2 t^{3}}{3} - 2 t^{2} - 2 t\\right)\\right]_{0}^{2}\\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{20 \\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\frac{20 \\sqrt{5}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720173 :question/problem "Evaluate the scalar line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(\\frac{1}{x - y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is given by $y=x + 2$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$." :question/worked-solution "Set $x=t$ and use the explicit equation to parametrize the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t + 2\\rangle,\\qquad -1\\le t\\le 1\n$$\n\nDifferentiate and calculate the nonnegative speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle1,1\\rangle\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(1\\right)^2}=\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=f\\left(t,t + 2\\right)=- \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nInclude the speed factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{-1}^{1} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplify the product before integrating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{\\sqrt{2} t}{2}\\right]_{-1}^{1}\\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720179 :question/problem "Let $p=\\langle2,0,-1\\rangle$, $q=\\langle-2,-3,-1\\rangle$, and $r=\\langle-1,0,-2\\rangle$. Find $r\\cdot(q\\times p)$ using a determinant." :question/worked-solution "The determinant rows follow the order in $r\\cdot(q\\times p)$: $r$, $q$, $p$.\n\n$$\nr\\cdot(q\\times p)=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nCall this determinant $D$. Expand across the first row with cofactor signs $+,-,+$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(-1)((-3)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0) \\\\ &\\quad -0((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 2) \\\\ &\\quad +(-2)((-2)\\cdot 0-(-3)\\cdot 2) \\\\ &=(-1)(3)-0(4)+(-2)(6) \\\\ &=-3+0-12 \\\\ &=-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720182 :question/problem "Let $a=\\langle-2,3,0\\rangle$, $b=\\langle-2,0,-1\\rangle$, and $c=\\langle3,1,-1\\rangle$. Find $a\\cdot(c\\times b)$ using a determinant." :question/worked-solution "The determinant rows follow the order in $a\\cdot(c\\times b)$: $a$, $c$, $b$.\n\n$$\na\\cdot(c\\times b)=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nCall this determinant $D$. Expand across the first row with cofactor signs $+,-,+$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(-2)(1\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0) \\\\ &\\quad -3(3\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\ &\\quad +0(3\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (-2)) \\\\ &=(-2)(-1)-3(-5)+0(2) \\\\ &=2+15+0 \\\\ &=17.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720186 :question/problem "Let $a=\\langle-2,3,2\\rangle$, $b=\\langle0,3,2\\rangle$, and $c=\\langle2,2,0\\rangle$. Find $b\\cdot(c\\times a)$ using a determinant.\n\n$b\\cdot(c\\times a)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The determinant rows follow the order in $b\\cdot(c\\times a)$: $b$, $c$, $a$.\n\n$$\nb\\cdot(c\\times a)=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nCall this determinant $D$. Expand across the first row with cofactor signs $+,-,+$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=0\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=0(2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 3) \\\\ &\\quad -3(2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-2)) \\\\ &\\quad +2(2\\cdot 3-2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\ &=0(4)-3(4)+2(10) \\\\ &=0-12+20 \\\\ &=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720188 :question/problem "Let $u=\\langle-3,-2,-1\\rangle$, $v=\\langle-3,0,1\\rangle$, and $w=\\langle3,0,-2\\rangle$. Find $w\\cdot(v\\times u)$ using a determinant.\n\n$w\\cdot(v\\times u)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The determinant rows follow the order in $w\\cdot(v\\times u)$: $w$, $v$, $u$.\n\n$$\nw\\cdot(v\\times u)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nCall this determinant $D$. Expand across the first row with cofactor signs $+,-,+$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=3(0\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-2)) \\\\ &\\quad -0((-3)\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-3)) \\\\ &\\quad +(-2)((-3)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot (-3)) \\\\ &=3(2)-0(6)+(-2)(6) \\\\ &=6+0-12 \\\\ &=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720193 :question/problem "Let $p=\\langle-3,-3,-2\\rangle$, $q=\\langle1,-3,0\\rangle$, and $r=\\langle0,2,-1\\rangle$. Find $r\\cdot(q\\times p)$ using a determinant.\n\n$r\\cdot(q\\times p)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The determinant rows follow the order in $r\\cdot(q\\times p)$: $r$, $q$, $p$.\n\n$$\nr\\cdot(q\\times p)=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nCall this determinant $D$. Expand across the first row with cofactor signs $+,-,+$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=0\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=0((-3)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot (-3)) \\\\ &\\quad -2(1\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot (-3)) \\\\ &\\quad +(-1)(1\\cdot (-3)-(-3)\\cdot (-3)) \\\\ &=0(6)-2(-2)+(-1)(-12) \\\\ &=0+4+12 \\\\ &=16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720204 :question/problem "Let $a=\\langle2,1,-2\\rangle$, $b=\\langle-3,1,2\\rangle$, and $c=\\langle-2,3,-3\\rangle$. Find $c\\cdot(a\\times b)$ using a determinant.\n\n$c\\cdot(a\\times b)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The determinant rows follow the order in $c\\cdot(a\\times b)$: $c$, $a$, $b$.\n\n$$\nc\\cdot(a\\times b)=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nCall this determinant $D$. Expand across the first row with cofactor signs $+,-,+$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=(-2)(1\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot 1) \\\\ &\\quad -3(2\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot (-3)) \\\\ &\\quad +(-3)(2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-3)) \\\\ &=(-2)(4)-3(-2)+(-3)(5) \\\\ &=-8+6-15 \\\\ &=-17.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720205 :question/problem "Let $u=\\langle2,2,1\\rangle$, $v=\\langle3,-1,2\\rangle$, and $w=\\langle-3,3,-3\\rangle$. Find $v\\cdot(w\\times u)$ using a determinant.\n\n$v\\cdot(w\\times u)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The determinant rows follow the order in $v\\cdot(w\\times u)$: $v$, $w$, $u$.\n\n$$\nv\\cdot(w\\times u)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nCall this determinant $D$. Expand across the first row with cofactor signs $+,-,+$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=3(3\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 2) \\\\ &\\quad -(-1)((-3)\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot 2) \\\\ &\\quad +2((-3)\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 2) \\\\ &=3(9)-(-1)(3)+2(-12) \\\\ &=27+3-24 \\\\ &=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720208 :question/problem "Let $u=\\langle3,-2,3\\rangle$, $v=\\langle2,-2,2\\rangle$, and $w=\\langle-1,1,-1\\rangle$. Find $u\\cdot(w\\times v)$ using a determinant.\n\n$u\\cdot(w\\times v)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The determinant rows follow the order in $u\\cdot(w\\times v)$: $u$, $w$, $v$.\n\n$$\nu\\cdot(w\\times v)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nCall this determinant $D$. Expand across the first row with cofactor signs $+,-,+$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=3(1\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\ &\\quad -(-2)((-1)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 2) \\\\ &\\quad +3((-1)\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 2) \\\\ &=3(0)-(-2)(0)+3(0) \\\\ &=0+0+0 \\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720210 :question/problem "Let $p=\\langle3,1,-1\\rangle$, $q=\\langle2,1,1\\rangle$, and $r=\\langle1,3,2\\rangle$. Find $r\\cdot(p\\times q)$ using a determinant.\n\n$r\\cdot(p\\times q)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The determinant rows follow the order in $r\\cdot(p\\times q)$: $r$, $p$, $q$.\n\n$$\nr\\cdot(p\\times q)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nCall this determinant $D$. Expand across the first row with cofactor signs $+,-,+$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1(1\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 1) \\\\ &\\quad -3(3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 2) \\\\ &\\quad +2(3\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 2) \\\\ &=1(2)-3(5)+2(1) \\\\ &=2-15+2 \\\\ &=-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720212 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\bigr)$, given $(\\sqrt{2}\\mathbf{a})\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{b}\\times (3\\mathbf{c})\\bigr)=2 \\sqrt{2}$.\n\n$\\mathbf{c}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\bigr)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Factor out the supplied scalar multipliers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{2}\\mathbf{a})\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{b}\\times (3\\mathbf{c})\\bigr)&=2 \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n3 \\sqrt{2}\\bigl[\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\bigr)\\bigr]&=2 \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\bigr)&=\\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSwap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$; one swap reverses the sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\bigr)&=- \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe requested scalar is $- \\frac{2}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720214 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\bigr)$, given $(3\\mathbf{b})\\cdot\\bigl((-3\\mathbf{c})\\times (-3\\mathbf{a})\\bigr)=- \\frac{81}{2}$.\n\n$\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\bigr)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Factor out the supplied scalar multipliers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(3\\mathbf{b})\\cdot\\bigl((-3\\mathbf{c})\\times (-3\\mathbf{a})\\bigr)&=- \\frac{81}{2} \\\\\n27\\bigl[\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\bigr)\\bigr]&=- \\frac{81}{2} \\\\\n\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\bigr)&=- \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSwap $\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{a}$; one swap reverses the sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\bigr)&=\\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe requested scalar is $\\frac{3}{2}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720219 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\bigr)$, given $(\\sqrt{2}\\mathbf{a})\\cdot\\bigl((-3\\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c}\\bigr)=- 9 \\sqrt{2}$.\n\n$\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\bigr)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Factor out the supplied scalar multipliers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{2}\\mathbf{a})\\cdot\\bigl((-3\\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c}\\bigr)&=- 9 \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n- 3 \\sqrt{2}\\bigl[\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\bigr)\\bigr]&=- 9 \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\bigr)&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSwap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$; one swap reverses the sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\bigr)&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSwap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$; one swap reverses the sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\bigr)&=3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe requested scalar is $3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720221 :question/problem "Suppose $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}=\\langle -3,1,-4\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 4,0,-2\\rangle$. Find $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\bigr)$.\n\n$\\mathbf{c}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\bigr)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First take the dot product with the remaining vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\bigr)&=\\langle 4,0,-2\\rangle\\cdot \\langle -3,1,-4\\rangle \\\\\n&=(4)\\cdot (-3)+(0)\\cdot (1)+(-2)\\cdot (-4) \\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSwap $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$; one swap reverses the sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\bigr)&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSwap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$; one swap reverses the sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\bigr)&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe requested scalar is $-4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720223 :question/problem "Suppose $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=\\langle 3,2,0\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 0,-1,-3\\rangle$. Find $\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\bigr)$.\n\n$\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\bigr)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First take the dot product with the remaining vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\bigr)&=\\langle 0,-1,-3\\rangle\\cdot \\langle 3,2,0\\rangle \\\\\n&=(0)\\cdot (3)+(-1)\\cdot (2)+(-3)\\cdot (0) \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSwap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$; one swap reverses the sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\bigl(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\bigr)&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe requested scalar is $2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720227 :question/problem "Calculate the area of triangle $PQR$ with vertices $P(1,-1,0)$, $Q(2,1,-1)$, and $R(0,0,2)$. Give the area in square units." :question/worked-solution "Form two side vectors with the same starting point:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{PQ}&=\\langle2-1,1-(-1),-1-0\\rangle=\\langle1,2,-1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{PR}&=\\langle0-1,0-(-1),2-0\\rangle=\\langle-1,1,2\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTake their cross product:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\times\\overrightarrow{PR}\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\1&2&-1\\\\-1&1&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=(2\\cdot2-(-1)\\cdot1)\\mathbf{i}\\\\\n&\\quad -(1\\cdot2-(-1)\\cdot(-1))\\mathbf{j}\\\\\n&\\quad +(1\\cdot1-2\\cdot(-1))\\mathbf{k}\\\\\n&=5\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}=\\langle5,-1,3\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe parallelogram's area is the magnitude of this vector:\n$$\n\\lVert\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\times\\overrightarrow{PR}\\rVert=\\sqrt{5^2+(-1)^2+3^2}=\\sqrt{35}.\n$$\nThe triangle occupies half the parallelogram, so\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720229 :question/problem "Triangle $ABC$ has vertices $A(1,1,1)$, $B(2,-1,3)$, and $C(3,2,0)$. Let $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b},\\mathbf{c}$ be their position vectors from the origin.\n\nCalculate the triangle's exact area in square units.\n\n$T=$ {{area}}\n\nAlso choose the expression below that gives this triangle's area." :question/worked-solution "Position vectors start at the origin. To get sides of this triangle, subtract the same vertex from the other two:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}&=\\langle2-1,-1-1,3-1\\rangle=\\langle1,-2,2\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a}&=\\langle3-1,2-1,0-1\\rangle=\\langle2,1,-1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus the correct expression is\n$$\nT=\\frac12\\lVert(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a})\\times(\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a})\\rVert.\n$$\nUsing $\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}$ would give an area based at the origin, not at $A$. A dot product does not give parallelogram area. The sum $\\mathbf{c}+\\mathbf{a}$ is not the side $\\overrightarrow{AC}$, and omitting the factor $\\frac12$ gives the parallelogram's area.\n\nNow compute:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a})\\times(\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a})\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\1&-2&2\\\\2&1&-1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=((-2)(-1)-2\\cdot1)\\mathbf{i}\\\\\n&\\quad -(1\\cdot(-1)-2\\cdot2)\\mathbf{j}\\\\\n&\\quad +(1\\cdot1-(-2)\\cdot2)\\mathbf{k}\\\\\n&=0\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}+5\\mathbf{k}=\\langle0,5,5\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIts magnitude is $\\sqrt{0^2+5^2+5^2}=\\sqrt{50}=5\\sqrt2$. Therefore,\n$$\nT=\\frac{5\\sqrt2}{2}.\n$$\nReversing the cross-product order reverses its direction but leaves this area unchanged."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720232 :question/problem "Calculate the area of triangle $ABC$ with vertices $A(1,0,1)$, $B(3,0,1)$, and $C(1,1,2)$.\n\nUse the two sides starting at $A$:\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=B-A,\\qquad \\mathbf{v}=C-A,\\qquad T=\\frac12\\lVert\\mathbf{u}\\times\\mathbf{v}\\rVert.\n$$\nThe diagram shows the parallelogram spanned by these sides, with $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{v}$. The triangle has half its area.\n\n![](images/27/2717ed1209f60dac12c3fcb07aea25f99c49b053fe0ce440b7ce996002a067c7.png)\n\nChoose the triangle's area in square units." :question/worked-solution "First subtract the coordinates of the common vertex:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{AB}&=\\langle3-1,0-0,1-1\\rangle=\\langle2,0,0\\rangle,\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{AC}&=\\langle1-1,1-0,2-1\\rangle=\\langle0,1,1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nCompute the cross product component by component:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{AB}\\times\\overrightarrow{AC}\n&=\\langle0\\cdot1-0\\cdot1,\\;0\\cdot0-2\\cdot1,\\;2\\cdot1-0\\cdot0\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle0,-2,2\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nIts magnitude is the parallelogram's area:\n$$\n\\lVert\\overrightarrow{AB}\\times\\overrightarrow{AC}\\rVert=\\sqrt{0^2+(-2)^2+2^2}=\\sqrt8=2\\sqrt2.\n$$\nThe triangle's area is half this value:\n$$\nT=\\frac12(2\\sqrt2)=\\sqrt2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720258 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, traversed in the direction of decreasing $t$, where\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,e^{t}\\rangle,\\,t\\in [1,2].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(9 x^{2} y^{3} - 4 x\\right)\\,i+\\left(9 x^{3} y^{2} - 6 y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Write $P=9 x^{2} y^{3} - 4 x$ and $Q=9 x^{3} y^{2} - 6 y.$ A potential must satisfy $f_x=P$ and $f_y=Q$. Integrating the first component with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3 x^{3} y^{3} - 2 x^{2}+g(y)\n$$\n\nMatch its $y$ derivative to the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 x^{3} y^{2}+g'(y)&=9 x^{3} y^{2} - 6 y \\\\ g'(y)&=- 6 y \\\\ g(y)&=- 3 y^{2}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $K=0$, since a constant cancels in the endpoint difference. Thus\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3 x^{3} y^{3} - 2 x^{2} - 3 y^{2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating verifies both components of the given field. For the stated orientation, the starting and ending points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A&=(2,e^{2}) \\\\ B&=(1,e)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the potential at each endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(B)&=3 \\left(1\\right)^{3} \\left(e\\right)^{3}- 2 \\left(1\\right)^{2}- 3 \\left(e\\right)^{2} \\\\ &=- 3 e^{2} - 2 + 3 e^{3} \\\\ f(A)&=3 \\left(2\\right)^{3} \\left(\\left(e^{2}\\right)\\right)^{3}- 2 \\left(2\\right)^{2}- 3 \\left(\\left(e^{2}\\right)\\right)^{2} \\\\ &=- 3 e^{4} - 8 + 24 e^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(B)-f(A) \\\\  & =\\left(- 3 e^{2} - 2 + 3 e^{3}\\right)-\\left(- 3 e^{4} - 8 + 24 e^{6}\\right) \\\\  & =- 24 e^{6} - 3 e^{2} + 6 + 3 e^{3} + 3 e^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720266 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, traversed in the direction of increasing $t$, where\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 3 \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)},\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(2 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 x\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Write $P=2 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 y$ and $Q=- x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 x.$ A potential must satisfy $f_x=P$ and $f_y=Q$. Integrating the first component with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 x y+g(y)\n$$\n\nMatch its $y$ derivative to the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 x+g'(y)&=- x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 x \\\\ g'(y)&=0 \\\\ g(y)&=0+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $K=0$, since a constant cancels in the endpoint difference. Thus\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 x y\n$$\n\nDifferentiating verifies both components of the given field. For the stated orientation, the starting and ending points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A&=(0,0) \\\\ B&=(0,\\frac{\\pi}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the potential at each endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(B)&=\\left(0\\right)^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}- 3 \\left(0\\right) \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\\\ &=0 \\\\ f(A)&=\\left(0\\right)^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}- 3 \\left(0\\right) \\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(B)-f(A) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720269 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, traversed in the direction of decreasing $t$, where\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t^{2} + 1,t\\rangle,\\,t\\in [\\pi,2 \\pi].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- 3 e^{x} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 e^{x} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Write $P=- 3 e^{x} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$ and $Q=3 e^{x} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.$ A potential must satisfy $f_x=P$ and $f_y=Q$. Integrating the first component with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- 3 e^{x} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}+g(y)\n$$\n\nMatch its $y$ derivative to the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 e^{x} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}+g'(y)&=3 e^{x} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} \\\\ g'(y)&=0 \\\\ g(y)&=0+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $K=0$, since a constant cancels in the endpoint difference. Thus\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- 3 e^{x} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating verifies both components of the given field. For the stated orientation, the starting and ending points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A&=(1 + 4 \\pi^{2},2 \\pi) \\\\ B&=(1 + \\pi^{2},\\pi)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the potential at each endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(B)&=- 3 e^{\\left(1 + \\pi^{2}\\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\left(\\pi\\right) \\right)} \\\\ &=3 e^{1 + \\pi^{2}} \\\\ f(A)&=- 3 e^{\\left(1 + 4 \\pi^{2}\\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\left(2 \\pi\\right) \\right)} \\\\ &=- 3 e^{1 + 4 \\pi^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(B)-f(A) \\\\  & =\\left(3 e^{1 + \\pi^{2}}\\right)-\\left(- 3 e^{1 + 4 \\pi^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =3 e^{1 + \\pi^{2}} + 3 e^{1 + 4 \\pi^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used $e^{u+v}=e^u e^v$ to factor the common exponential."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720274 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, traversed in the direction of decreasing $t$, where\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 3 t,t^{2} - 2\\rangle,\\,t\\in [1,2].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- 6 x y + 2 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 3 x^{2} + 2 x\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Write $P=- 6 x y + 2 y$ and $Q=- 3 x^{2} + 2 x.$ A potential must satisfy $f_x=P$ and $f_y=Q$. Integrating the first component with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- 3 x^{2} y + 2 x y+g(y)\n$$\n\nMatch its $y$ derivative to the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 3 x^{2} + 2 x+g'(y)&=- 3 x^{2} + 2 x \\\\ g'(y)&=0 \\\\ g(y)&=0+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $K=0$, since a constant cancels in the endpoint difference. Thus\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- 3 x^{2} y + 2 x y\n$$\n\nDifferentiating verifies both components of the given field. For the stated orientation, the starting and ending points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A&=(6,2) \\\\ B&=(3,-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the potential at each endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(B)&=- 3 \\left(3\\right)^{2} \\left(-1\\right)+2 \\left(3\\right) \\left(-1\\right) \\\\ &=21 \\\\ f(A)&=- 3 \\left(6\\right)^{2} \\left(2\\right)+2 \\left(6\\right) \\left(2\\right) \\\\ &=-192\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(B)-f(A) \\\\  & =\\left(21\\right)-\\left(-192\\right) \\\\  & =213.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720281 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, traversed in the direction of increasing $t$, where\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},t^{2}\\rangle,\\,t\\in [2,3].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- 3 x^{2} - 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 3 x - 2 y^{2}\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Write $P=- 3 x^{2} - 3 y$ and $Q=- 3 x - 2 y^{2}.$ A potential must satisfy $f_x=P$ and $f_y=Q$. Integrating the first component with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- x^{3} - 3 x y+g(y)\n$$\n\nMatch its $y$ derivative to the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 3 x+g'(y)&=- 3 x - 2 y^{2} \\\\ g'(y)&=- 2 y^{2} \\\\ g(y)&=- \\frac{2 y^{3}}{3}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $K=0$, since a constant cancels in the endpoint difference. Thus\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- x^{3} - 3 x y - \\frac{2 y^{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating verifies both components of the given field. For the stated orientation, the starting and ending points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A&=(\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)},4) \\\\ B&=(\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)},9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the potential at each endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(B)&=- \\left(\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}\\right)^{3}- \\frac{2 \\left(9\\right)^{3}}{3}- 3 \\left(\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}\\right) \\left(9\\right) \\\\ &=-486 - 27 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} - \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}^{3} \\\\ f(A)&=- \\left(\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\right)^{3}- \\frac{2 \\left(4\\right)^{3}}{3}- 3 \\left(\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\right) \\left(4\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{128}{3} - 12 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} - \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(B)-f(A) \\\\  & =\\left(-486 - 27 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} - \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}^{3}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{128}{3} - 12 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} - \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}^{3}\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{1330}{3} - 27 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} - \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}^{3} + \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}^{3} + 12 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720290 :question/problem "The vertices of $\\triangle ABC$ are $A(1,0,1)$, $B(3,2,1)$, and $C(2,1,3)$. Let $H$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $C$ to the line $AB$.\n\nThe following values have been calculated from the vertices:\n\n$$\nAB=2\\sqrt{2},\\qquad \\left|\\overrightarrow{AB}\\times\\overrightarrow{AC}\\right|=4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nUse the area relationship\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle ABC}=\\frac12\\left|\\overrightarrow{AB}\\times\\overrightarrow{AC}\\right|=\\frac12 AB\\cdot CH\n$$\n\nto calculate the length of the altitude $CH$." :question/worked-solution "The cross-product magnitude is the area of the parallelogram, so the triangle has area\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle ABC}=\\frac12(4\\sqrt{2})=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThe altitude $CH$ corresponds to the base $AB$. Substituting into the triangle area formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sqrt{2}&=\\frac12(2\\sqrt{2})CH\\\\\n2\\sqrt{2}&=\\sqrt{2}\\,CH\\\\\nCH&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe altitude is $2$ units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720298 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $MH$ of $\\triangle LMN$, where $L(1,1,1)$, $M(3,0,2)$, and $N(2,3,3)$. Here $H$ is the foot of the perpendicular from $M$ to the line $LN$." :question/worked-solution "The altitude $MH$ corresponds to the base $LN$, so\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle LMN}=\\frac12 LN\\cdot MH.\n$$\n\nFirst form two edge vectors with the same starting point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{LN}&=\\langle 2-1,3-1,3-1\\rangle=\\langle 1,2,2\\rangle,\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{LM}&=\\langle 3-1,0-1,2-1\\rangle=\\langle 2,-1,1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate their cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{LN}\\times\\overrightarrow{LM}\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\1&2&2\\\\2&-1&1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=(2\\cdot1-2\\cdot(-1))\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad -(1\\cdot1-2\\cdot2)\\mathbf j\\\\\n&\\quad +(1\\cdot(-1)-2\\cdot2)\\mathbf k\\\\\n&=4\\mathbf i+3\\mathbf j-5\\mathbf k\\\\\n&=\\langle 4,3,-5\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts magnitude gives the parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\left|\\overrightarrow{LN}\\times\\overrightarrow{LM}\\right|=\\sqrt{4^2+3^2+(-5)^2}=\\sqrt{50}=5\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore the triangle area and the length of the corresponding base are\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle LMN}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}2,\\qquad LN=\\sqrt{1^2+2^2+2^2}=3.\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the area formula and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}2&=\\frac12(3)MH,\\\\\n5\\sqrt{2}&=3MH,\\\\\nMH&=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe altitude is $\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{2}}3$ units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720306 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $RH$ of $\\triangle PQR$, where $P(1,-1,2)$, $Q(3,0,4)$, and $R(2,1,2)$. Here $H$ is the foot of the perpendicular from $R$ to the line $PQ$.\n\nTo help you, the cross product has been calculated:\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\times\\overrightarrow{PR}=\\langle -4,2,3\\rangle.\n$$\n\nIts magnitude is the area of the parallelogram spanned by the two edge vectors." :question/worked-solution "Since the altitude is drawn from $R$ to the line $PQ$, its corresponding base is $PQ$. From the vertices,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{PQ}&=\\langle 3-1,0-(-1),4-2\\rangle=\\langle 2,1,2\\rangle,\\\\\nPQ&=\\sqrt{2^2+1^2+2^2}=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe supplied cross product has magnitude\n\n$$\n\\left|\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\times\\overrightarrow{PR}\\right|=\\sqrt{(-4)^2+2^2+3^2}=\\sqrt{29}.\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half the area of that parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle PQR}=\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the matching base and altitude,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle PQR}&=\\frac12 PQ\\cdot RH,\\\\\n\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}2&=\\frac12(3)RH,\\\\\nRH&=\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe altitude is $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{29}}3$ units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720309 :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}&=\\langle1,2,1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{b}&=\\langle-1,1,2\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\langle2,-1,1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped whose three edge vectors are $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$, $\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}$, and $\\mathbf{c}$.\n\n$V=${{volume}}" :question/worked-solution "First form the actual edge vectors component by component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{u}=\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}&=\\langle 1+(-1),2+1,1+2\\rangle=\\langle0,3,3\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}&=\\langle 1-(-1),2-1,1-2\\rangle=\\langle2,1,-1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{c}&=\\langle2,-1,1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe volume is $V=|\\mathbf{u}\\cdot(\\mathbf{v}\\times\\mathbf{w})|$. Use these edge vectors, rather than $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b},\\mathbf{c}$, as the determinant rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot(\\mathbf{v}\\times\\mathbf{w})\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}0&3&3\\\\2&1&-1\\\\2&-1&1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=0-3\\begin{vmatrix}2&-1\\\\2&1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}2&1\\\\2&-1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=-3(2\\cdot1-(-1)\\cdot2)+3(2(-1)-1\\cdot2)\\\\\n&=-3(4)+3(-4)\\\\\n&=-24.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the absolute value gives\n\n$$\nV=|-24|=24.\n$$\n\nThe two mixed edges are not parallel simply because both are made from $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$. Subtracting the vectors also requires subtracting each signed component."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720316 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 1,2,0\\rangle$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 0,1,2\\rangle$, and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle -3,0,1\\rangle$.\n\n![A parallelepiped with three edge vectors a, b, and c](images/25/2597b48a010607fca71c551dbce8e9e2bbeda47d0892a582347ae1cdf19f1bf6.png)\n\nThe diagram is schematic and is not to scale." :question/worked-solution "The volume is the absolute value of the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\nV=|\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})|.\n$$\n\nUse the edge vectors as the rows of a determinant. Expanding along the first row gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}1&2&0\\\\0&1&2\\\\-3&0&1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=1\\begin{vmatrix}1&2\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0&2\\\\-3&1\\end{vmatrix}+0\\\\\n&=1(1\\cdot1-2\\cdot0)-2(0\\cdot1-2(-3))\\\\\n&=1-12=-11.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triple product is signed, but volume is nonnegative. Therefore\n\n$$\nV=|-11|=11.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720319 :question/problem "Suppose $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})=-5$. Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped whose three edge vectors are $2\\mathbf{b}$, $\\mathbf{c}$, and $3\\mathbf{a}$.\n\n$V=${{volume}}" :question/worked-solution "The volume is the absolute value of the scalar triple product of the actual edge vectors:\n\n$$\nV=|(2\\mathbf{b})\\cdot(\\mathbf{c}\\times3\\mathbf{a})|.\n$$\n\nFactor out the two scale factors. A cyclic change from $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b},\\mathbf{c}$ to $\\mathbf{b},\\mathbf{c},\\mathbf{a}$ preserves the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2\\mathbf{b})\\cdot(\\mathbf{c}\\times3\\mathbf{a})\n&=6\\,\\mathbf{b}\\cdot(\\mathbf{c}\\times\\mathbf{a})\\\\\n&=6\\,\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})\\\\\n&=6(-5)=-30.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, take the absolute value:\n\n$$\nV=|-30|=30.\n$$\n\nScaling two different edges multiplies their scale factors; it does not add or square them."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720321 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle k,1,1\\rangle$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 1,0,1\\rangle$, and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle 0,1,2\\rangle$ are coplanar.\n\nThe first-row expansion of their scalar triple product is provided:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})&=k(0\\cdot2-1\\cdot1)\\\\\n&\\quad -(1\\cdot2-1\\cdot0)\\\\\n&\\quad +(1\\cdot1-0\\cdot0).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the value of $k$." :question/worked-solution "Three vectors are coplanar if and only if their scalar triple product is zero. Simplify the supplied expansion:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})&=k(0-1)-(2-0)+(1-0)\\\\\n&=-k-2+1\\\\\n&=-k-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet this equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-k-1&=0\\\\\n-k&=1\\\\\nk&=-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the vectors are coplanar exactly when $k=-1$. At this value the triple product is $-(-1)-1=0$, so the parallelepiped spanned by the vectors has zero volume."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720332 :question/problem "Find $t$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle t,2,t\\rangle$, $\\mathbf{q}=\\langle 1,2,-1\\rangle$, and $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle 2,1,3\\rangle$ are coplanar.\n\n$t=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The vectors are coplanar if and only if $\\mathbf{p}\\cdot(\\mathbf{q}\\times\\mathbf{r})=0$. Compute the triple product as a determinant, expanding along its first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{p}\\cdot(\\mathbf{q}\\times\\mathbf{r})\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}t&2&t\\\\1&2&-1\\\\2&1&3\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=t\\begin{vmatrix}2&-1\\\\1&3\\end{vmatrix}\n-2\\begin{vmatrix}1&-1\\\\2&3\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad +t\\begin{vmatrix}1&2\\\\2&1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=t(2\\cdot3-(-1)\\cdot1)-2(1\\cdot3-(-1)\\cdot2)\\\\\n&\\quad +t(1\\cdot1-2\\cdot2)\\\\\n&=7t-10-3t\\\\\n&=4t-10.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth occurrences of $t$ contribute to the equation. Setting the triple product equal to zero gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4t-10&=0\\\\\n4t&=10\\\\\nt&=\\frac{5}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vectors are coplanar exactly when $t=\\frac{5}{2}$. Substitution gives $4\\cdot\\frac{5}{2}-10=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720333 :question/problem "Find all real values of $k$ for which the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 3,k^2,3\\rangle$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 1,3,2\\rangle$, and $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle 2,-1,1\\rangle$ are coplanar.\n\nEnter the two values in ascending order, using exact values.\n\n$k=${{field-1}} and $k=${{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Three vectors are coplanar if and only if their scalar triple product is zero. Expanding along the first row gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot(\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c})\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}3&k^2&3\\\\1&3&2\\\\2&-1&1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=3\\begin{vmatrix}3&2\\\\-1&1\\end{vmatrix}\n-k^2\\begin{vmatrix}1&2\\\\2&1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&\\quad +3\\begin{vmatrix}1&3\\\\2&-1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=3(3\\cdot1-2\\cdot(-1))-k^2(1\\cdot1-2\\cdot2)\\\\\n&\\quad +3(1\\cdot(-1)-3\\cdot2)\\\\\n&=3(5)-k^2(-3)+3(-7)\\\\\n&=15+3k^2-21\\\\\n&=3k^2-6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet the triple product equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3k^2-6&=0\\\\\n3k^2&=6\\\\\nk^2&=2\\\\\nk&=\\pm\\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth signs must be retained because either value has square $2$. In ascending order, the values are $-\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$. For each, the triple product is $3(2)-6=0$, so both make the vectors coplanar."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720338 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=x^{3}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 2$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Set $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,t^{3}\\rangle,\\qquad -1\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,3 t^{2}\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(3 t^{2}\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=t \\left(t^{3}\\right)\\\\\n&=t^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-1}^{2} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(t^{4}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(3 t^{2}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720350 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ for $1\\le x\\le 3$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- 2 x^{3} y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Set $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,\\frac{1}{t}\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=- 2 t^{3} \\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 t^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{1}^{3} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 2 t^{2}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720351 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=2 e^{x}$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Set $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,2 e^{t}\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,2 e^{t}\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(2 e^{t}\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\left(2 e^{t}\\right) \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\\n&=2 e^{t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{1}^{2} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(2 e^{t} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(2 e^{t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720355 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=3 x + 1$ for $-1\\le x\\le 2$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(y e^{x}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Set $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,3 t + 1\\rangle,\\qquad -1\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,3\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(3\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\left(3 t + 1\\right) e^{t}\\\\\n&=\\left(3 t + 1\\right) e^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-1}^{2} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{2}\\left(\\left(3 t + 1\\right) e^{t}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(3\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720367 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=e^{x}$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- 2 y e^{- x}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Set $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,e^{t}\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,e^{t}\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(e^{t}\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=- 2 \\left(e^{t}\\right) e^{- t}\\\\\n&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe exponential factors cancel: $e^t e^{-t}=1$.\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{1}^{2} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(-2\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(e^{t}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720374 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 2 \\right)}$ with $x>0$ between its intersections with the horizontal lines $y=\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}$ and $y=\\ln{\\left(11 \\right)}$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- 2 x e^{y} - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "First solve the intersection equations on the specified branch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln(x^{2} + 2)=\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}&\\implies x^{2} + 2=3\\implies x=1\\\\\n\\ln(x^{2} + 2)=\\ln{\\left(11 \\right)}&\\implies x^{2} + 2=11\\implies x=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le 3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=- 2 t e^{\\left(\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)} - 2 \\left(\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 t^{3} - 4 t - 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $e^{\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}}=t^{2} + 2$ because the logarithm argument is positive on the interval.\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{1}^{3} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- 2 t^{3} - 4 t - 2 \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 2 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 2}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720383 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the section of $y=\\ln{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}$ with $x<0$ between its intersections with the horizontal lines $y=\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}$ and $y=\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}$.\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(x e^{y} + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\n\nusing $x=t$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "First solve the intersection equations on the specified branch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln(x^{2} + 1)=\\ln{\\left(5 \\right)}&\\implies x^{2} + 1=5\\implies x=-2\\\\\n\\ln(x^{2} + 1)=\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}&\\implies x^{2} + 1=2\\implies x=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSet $x=t$. Parametrize the curve by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad -2\\le t\\le -1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate both coordinates and take the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\langle 1,\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 1}\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 1}\\right)^2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=t e^{\\left(\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\right)} + \\left(\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=t^{3} + t + \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 1 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $e^{\\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 1 \\right)}}=t^{2} + 1$ because the logarithm argument is positive on the interval.\n\nMultiply the scalar by the speed and use the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{-2}^{-1} f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r\\,\\!{}^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the required setup is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\left(t^{3} + t + \\ln{\\left(t^{2} + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\,\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{2 t}{t^{2} + 1}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}]}
